# bG fitted to X AND Y together, at the noise-ACF shaper. DIAGNOSIS, 2026-10-14.
# FRESH_LOOK §16.2: bG 0.45 (fitted to X alone) overshoots Y (data/sim 0.69-0.76); a rough
# 1/(1-bG) estimate puts a joint X+Y fit near 0.35. §10.2: the target run's noise ACF says
# the 180 ns code shapes like ~166 ns on FEU 7/8. New digitizer switch --shaper-tp-ns.
# Scan: s = 1.46 / 1.75 x bG = 0.25 / 0.35 / 0.45 x {th00, thx10, thx20}, all at tp 166 ns.
# The bG 0.45 points pair with cluster 4450899 (same seed, tp 180) and isolate the shaper.
#
# PRE-REGISTERED (2026-10-14, before submission), against the T14 harness of §16:
#   * shaper alone (bG 0.45, tp 166 vs 180): inclined X rise -6 to -12 ns, FWHM -10 to -20 ns;
#     peak amplitude data/sim up by 2-6 % (faster shaper integrates less of the ion tail).
#   * bG: peak-amplitude medians scale less than the mean 1/(1-bG) (ratio 0.85 / 0.73 for
#     0.35 / 0.25 vs 0.45) but by at least 2/3 of it. Expected data/sim at bG 0.35:
#     X vertical ~0.9-1.0, X inclined ~1.0-1.25, Y ~0.8-0.95. The joint X+Y optimum lands
#     at bG 0.30-0.40; at that point X and Y still disagree by 10-20 % (the §16.2 split is
#     not a bG effect).
#   * rise/FWHM move by < 5 ns between bG values at fixed s and tp (feedback is prompt).
# FALSIFIER: shape moves > 5 ns with bG at fixed tp, uncensored -> feedback not shape-neutral;
#   the X/Y amplitude disagreement vanishes at some bG -> it was a gain effect after all and
#   charge division is not needed to explain it.
